.\" dux - Quick directory size overview
.\" Man page for dux (dir-sizes)
.TH DUX 1 "November 2026" "dux 1.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dux \- quick directory size overview
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dux
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIDIRECTORY\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dux
displays the size of each subdirectory within the specified directory,
sorted from smallest to largest. It provides a quick overview of disk
space distribution, making it easy to identify which directories are
consuming the most space.
.PP
The largest directories appear at the bottom of the output for easy
viewing in a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-L
Follow all symbolic links: symlinked subdirectories are listed and sized
as their targets. Without this option only a symlinked starting directory
is followed, and symlinked subdirectories are not listed.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-one\-file\-system
Stay on the target's filesystem: subdirectories that are mount points of
other filesystems are left out, and sizes do not count other filesystems
mounted below (as \fBdu \-x\fR). Useful for \fBsudo dux /\fR, which would
otherwise include \fI/proc\fR and network mounts.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress permission error messages. Errors from \fBdu\fR(1) are
redirected to /dev/null instead of stderr. Useful when scanning
directories with mixed permissions.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (too many arguments).
.TP
.B 3
Directory not found.
.TP
.B 5
I/O error: \fBdu\fR(1) returned no size for a directory that still exists.
.TP
.B 22
Invalid command-line option.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
Output is tab-separated with two columns:
.PP
.RS
\fIsize\fR	\fIpath\fR
.RE
.PP
Sizes use IEC units (B, KiB, MiB, GiB, TiB) with one decimal place.
The output is sorted by size, smallest to largest.
.SH EXAMPLES
Analyze the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.B dux
.RE
.PP
Check /var subdirectories:
.PP
.RS
.B dux /var
.RE
.PP
Show the 5 largest directories in home:
.PP
.RS
.B dux ~ | tail -5
.RE
.PP
Full system scan (requires root):
.PP
.RS
.B sudo dux /
.RE
.PP
Suppress permission errors:
.PP
.RS
.B dux -q /var
.RE
.PP
Follow symlinks:
.PP
.RS
.B dux -L /opt/myapp
.RE
.PP
Combine options:
.PP
.RS
.B dux -Lq /some/path
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B dux
sets a restricted PATH for security:
.PP
.RS
/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B dux
uses GNU \fBdu \-sb\fR to calculate apparent size in bytes for each
subdirectory, running one \fBdu\fR per directory in parallel (one job per
CPU, via \fBxargs\fR(1)). A file hard-linked into several subdirectories
is therefore counted in full for each of them. Permission errors are reported to stderr (unless \fB-q\fR
is used) but do not stop execution; accessible content is still measured
and displayed.
.PP
Sizes are apparent sizes (bytes of content, as \fBdu \-b\fR), not
allocated disk blocks, so a sparse file counts at its full length.
.PP
A subdirectory that disappears between listing and sizing (a process
directory under \fI/proc\fR, a temporary directory) is left out of the
output; the error from \fBdu\fR still appears on stderr unless \fB-q\fR
is used.
.PP
Empty directories show as 0.0B (just directory metadata).
.PP
Sizes always use a full stop as the decimal point, whatever the locale.
.PP
No temporary files are created; sizes are sorted and formatted in a pipeline.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Output is one line per directory, so a directory name containing a newline
is printed across two lines.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR du (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR ncdu (1),
.BR find (1)
